{"term": "костыль", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "kostyl'", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "penis", "concept": "penis", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 30, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'crutch' or 'walking cane'; a humorous colloquial metaphor for the penis, sometimes implying an awkward, large, or age-related quality.", "english_term": "crutch", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The crutch metaphor is inherently absurdist; less common than other shape-based terms; the word's primary medical meaning (mobility aid) creates a comic or slightly self-deprecating image when applied to the anatomy; occasionally implies erectile difficulty."}
{"term": "ялда", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "yalda", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "penis", "concept": "penis", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 70, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A crude slang term for the penis, possibly of Turkic or Ottoman origin, found in Russian obscene vocabulary as an alternative to хуй.", "english_term": "cock", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Etymology is debated; likely entered Russian through historical contact with Turkic-speaking populations; cruder than neutral slang but occupies a lower vulgarity tier than core mat terms; recognition may skew higher among older speakers and those familiar with literary or folkloric obscenity."}
{"term": "дрочилка", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "drochilka", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "penis", "concept": "penis", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 68, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 60, "recognition_pct_peak": 60, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Derived from дрочить (to masturbate); used crudely as a nickname for the penis in the sense of 'thing one jerks off with'; also applied as a general insult.", "english_term": "wanker", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The -илка suffix denotes an instrument or agent noun; derivation from the masturbation verb makes the organ reference especially crude; occasionally extended as a general insult for a contemptible person, paralleling English 'wanker' though the organ-name primary meaning is more prominent."}
{"term": "влагалище", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "vlagalishche", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "vagina", "concept": "vagina", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 4, "technical": 95, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The standard clinical and anatomical Russian term for the vagina; the internal muscular canal extending from the cervix to the vulvar opening.", "english_term": "vagina", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard term in medical, educational, and formal discourse; its Slavic etymology (from влагать, 'to insert') is more semantically transparent than the Latin 'vagina'; widely taught in school biology; not vulgar but rarely used in casual speech outside educational or medical contexts."}
{"term": "вульва", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "vul'va", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "vagina", "concept": "vulva", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 4, "technical": 95, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The standard clinical Russian term for the vulva; the collective name for the external female genitalia including the labia, clitoris, and vaginal opening.", "english_term": "vulva", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "A Latin loanword used in Russian clinical and anatomical contexts; less commonly recognized by the general public than влагалище; public health campaigns and feminist educational initiatives have increased recognition in recent decades; the anatomical distinction from влагалище is frequently conflated in lay speech."}
{"term": "половая щель", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "polovaya shchel'", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "vagina", "concept": "vulva", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 12, "technical": 72, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'genital slit' or 'genital cleft'; a semi-clinical term referring to the visible external cleft of the vulva (the rima pudendi), used in medical and sexological texts.", "english_term": "genital cleft", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Refers specifically to the external cleft rather than the internal canal; found in clinical textbooks and sexology literature; the word щель ('slit') gives the phrase a mildly explicit edge compared to other clinical terms, making it sound slightly more graphic to lay ears despite its technical usage."}
{"term": "женские половые органы", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "zhenskie polovye organy", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "vagina", "concept": "genitalia", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 3, "technical": 88, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'female sexual organs'; the standard formal Russian phrase encompassing all female genitalia, both external and internal, used in medical, legal, and educational contexts.", "english_term": "female genitalia", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "A multi-word noun phrase rather than a single lexical item; the standard choice in formal writing, health education curricula, and legal documents; recognized virtually universally by literate adult Russian speakers; the phrase's comprehensive scope (internal and external) distinguishes it from вульва or влагалище."}
{"term": "половые губы", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "polovye guby", "category": "organs", "subcategory": "vagina", "concept": "labia", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 82, "recognition_pct_current": 83, "recognition_pct_peak": 83, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'genital lips'; the standard clinical Russian term for the labia collectively (both majora and minora), the lip-like folds of skin forming the outer and inner boundaries of the vulva.", "english_term": "labia", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "A calque translation of the Latin 'labia' that is transparently descriptive in Russian; used in both clinical settings and general health education; more widely recognized than the Latin loanword лабии; used collectively when no distinction between majora and minora is required."}
